WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.270
Joker definitely goes its own way with the
depiction of the legendary Clown of Prince

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:07.700
of Crime, right up to its explosive and unforgettable
climax.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.280
Now that everyone's had a chance to see the
film, it's time to talk about what it all

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.060
built to and how it all paid off.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.440
Spoilers ahead for the entire film!

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.520
The whole of Joker is about the transformation
of Arthur Fleck from downtrodden, beat-up

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.360
loser to hero in his own mind sometimes literally
in his own mind.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.190
The last time we see Arthur he's walking out
of an interview room in Arkham State Hospital,

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.830
fresh off the triumph of the night he shot
Murray Franklin and became the figurehead

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:34.420
of an entire riotous movement.

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:38.980
We see Arthur leave bloody footprints as he
dances down the hall, and then we see an orderly

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.030
chasing him down the corridors, suggesting
a violent escape might be in order for the

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.860
newly minted Clown Prince of Crime.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:49.200
The film makes it clear several times that
Arthur Fleck is a man who has no trouble losing

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:53.730
himself in elaborate fantasies, but the question
of what's fact or fantasy doesn't actually

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:57.899
change the outcome for Arthur Fleck by the
end of Joker, whether he's escaping to a new

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:02.420
life of crime or caught in a delusion, it's
clear that he's long since passed the point

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:03.340
of no return.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.120
“Ha.”

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:11.800
One of the most important structural aspects
of Joker is just how small Arthur's inner

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:12.520
circle is.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:16.700
He has his mother, of course, and he seemingly
has friends at the clown company until they

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:21.000
begin to turn on him, but apart from them
the only major presence left is Sophie, his

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.220
neighbor who we actually know less about than
some parts of the film would have us believe.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.160
In Arthur's mind, Sophie is his girlfriend,
at least until she isn't.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:32.890
In Sophie's mind, Arthur is just the slightly
strange neighbor she and her daughter ride

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.160
the elevator with sometimes.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.720
When he walks into her apartment uninvited
and we discover his fantasy life with her,

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.880
she is clearly scared but also eager to defuse
the situation.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:43.660
So, what becomes of Sophie?

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.900
It's hard to say, but what's clear is that
the police will soon be finding a brutally

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:52.869
murdered body in Arthur's apartment, so they'll
probably be knocking on her door to ask questions,

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:55.680
at which point she'll discover just how lucky
she was.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.299
Throughout the film, as Arthur goes through
his own deeply personal descent into violent,

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.340
vengeful madness, a massive groundswell of
support for him is building right under his

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:04.840
nose.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.820
Near the end of the film, Arthur promises
Murray Franklin that he's apolitical.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:13.120
He says that he has nothing to do with the
protestors who've adopted clown masks in solidarity

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.310
with the then-anonymous subway shooter, but
he's clearly pleased by the idea that people

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:18.980
have begun to idolize him in some way.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.770
By the end of the film Arthur has fully embraced
the people who have become his followers,

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.650
standing up among them as a leader for one
triumphant moment before the film cuts to

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:27.160
him in Arkham.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:32.640
So whether Arthur stays in Arkham State Hospital
or not, the Joker Gang will simply not calm

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:33.520
down and go home.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:38.280
They might dissipate, or relax their protests
a bit and eventually fade out, but that doesn't

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.440
just go away because the Joker is in jail.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.620
He was simply the face they adopted for their
own social unrest.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:47.880
The gang is still out there, and they might
still be causing vast amounts of chaos.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.700
Arthur's explorations of his mother's true
motives as she obsessively writes letters

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.340
to Thomas Wayne take him on an emotional rollercoaster.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:00.060
He initially only sees Wayne as his mother's
former employer, then Penny confesses that

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.220
Wayne is his father.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.080
Thomas Wayne and his butler Alfred
deny this, and Arthur later seemingly confirms

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.160
their denials after stealing his mother's
records from Arkham and confirming that he

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:09.960
was adopted.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:11.480
But is that the whole truth?

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.640
After he kills his mother, Arthur finds an
old photo of her with an inscription on the

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.320
back signed by "T.W."

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.080
So perhaps Thomas Wayne is not Arthur's father,
but had an affair with Arthur's adoptive mother?

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:26.340
Or did it go down like Penny insisted the
whole adoption story was a coverup for what

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:27.200
really happened?

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.040
We can't say for sure, but that little inscription
suggests that there's more to the story than

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.780
those Arkham files had to offer.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.140
Joker spends much of its runtime dodging potential
comic book connections.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.260
When those connections do kick in, though,
they lead down a dark path, a chain reaction

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.440
started by Arthur that ends in the murder
of Thomas and Martha Wayne.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.200
One of the final images of the film is Bruce
Wayne standing alone in that same alley, the

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:52.100
bodies of his parents on either side of him.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.280
It's an image that Arthur laughs at, and the
film suggests that he'll hold onto that image

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.380
going forward as Bruce Wayne grows up.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:02.540
The big question that the film doesn't answer,
though, is how seeing a clown shoot his parents

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:04.060
will end up changing Bruce.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.360
Batman is usually depicted as a guy who fights
criminals in general, but this version of

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:12.420
Bruce might end up having a very specific
agenda on his hands when he's all grown up,

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.680
one that would fundamentally alter the Dark
Knight as we know him.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:21.420
Very early on in Joker, when Arthur goes home
to relax in front of the TV with his mother

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:25.620
at the end of the day, we see the first traces
of the fantasy life he leads as he imagines

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.340
himself in the studio audience of Murray Franklin's
show.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.060
Later, the film shows us that this fantasy
can go much, much deeper.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:37.020
This is all important because it emphasizes
just how lonely and unstable Arthur is.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.780
It's also important because it forces us to
contend with the possibility that at least

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:44.540
some of what Arthur does during the film's
climax didn't actually happen.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:48.140
Was he really carried out of the cop car by
his followers after the crash?

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:50.040
Did he really murder his therapist?

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:51.120
Does it go deeper?

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.540
Was he really in the asylum the whole time,
just imagining all of this?

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:58.500
Is that why the color of his clothes always
matches Arkham's decor?

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.340
These are questions that encourage repeat
viewing of the film, to see just how many

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.440
details point to the reality or fantasy of
each scenario.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.120
Joker is, among many other things, the origin
story for a villain, even if it's not the

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.680
same version of the villain we're used to
seeing on the comic book page or even on the

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:15.320
big screen.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:19.420
By the end of the film, Arthur Fleck is directly
asking people to call him Joker, and drawing

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:22.840
a big smile on his face in his own blood seems
to seal the deal.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:24.200
He's the Joker now.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.540
"When you bring me out, can you introduce
me as Joker?"

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:33.080
But there's also no mystery over the Joker's
identity this time around, so how can we reconcile

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:34.540
him with the comic book version?

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:38.840
Well, the short answer is we don't have to,
and the film doesn't really want us to, but

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:42.460
if you're really interested in digging that
deep there are a couple of possibilities.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.900
One is that this is just another version of
the many fake origin stories that Joker likes

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:48.440
to throw around to confuse people.

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:52.900
The other is that Arthur Fleck is not the
only "Joker," and that one day, perhaps when

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:57.300
Bruce Wayne is all grown up, a mystery man
will adopt the moniker with Arthur as the

00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:57.940
inspiration.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.060
Then again, maybe it was all a dream.

